 ## Overview
-ExBluRF is a novel view synthesis method designed to reconstruct sharp 3D scenes from extreme motion-blurred images by jointly optimizing voxel-based radiance fields and 6-DOF camera trajectories [S1]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [FastNeRF](7183-fastnerf.md) and [C-NeRF](7275-c-nerf.md), which focus on static scene acceleration, by addressing the ill-posed problem of deblurring through a trajectory-based blur model and memory-efficient voxel representations, unlike [DeblurNeRF](7183-fastnerf.md) which relies on spatially varying 2D kernels and MLPs [S1].
+ExBluRF is a novel view synthesis method designed to reconstruct sharp 3D scenes from extreme motion-blurred images by jointly optimizing voxel-based radiance fields and 6-DOF camera trajectories [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like [FastNeRF](7183-fastnerf.md) or [C-NeRF](7275-c-nerf.md) which focus on static scene acceleration, and from [DeblurNeRF](10878-rendernet.md) (a sibling in the broader deblurring context, though not listed in the immediate sibling tree, it is the primary comparison in [S1]) which uses 2D pixel-wise blur kernels, by formulating motion blur as a trajectory-based accumulation of rays along a Bézier curve [S1]. This approach allows ExBluRF to handle extreme blur with significantly lower memory and computational costs compared to MLP-based deblurring methods, as its memory complexity remains constant relative to the number of blur samples [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of $V$ extremely motion-blurred images $\{I_v\}_{v=1}^V$ with known initial camera poses (extrinsics $R_v, K_v$) and shutter times $[\tau_o, \tau_c]$.
-**Outputs:** A voxel-based radiance field (density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$) and optimized 6-DOF camera trajectories for each view.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
